Sure! Those numbers point to a spot in Mountain View, California – specifically the Googleplex (Google’s headquarters). If you’d like to verify or retrieve more details (like the full address, place ID, etc.) you can use the Google Geocoding API’s **reverse‑geocoding** endpoint.

Below is an example of how you would query the API:

```http
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=37.4224764,-122.0842499&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual Google Maps API key.  
When you send this request, the JSON response will contain information such as:

| Field | Example Value |
|-------|---------------|
| `formatted_address` | “1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA” |
| `place_id` | “ChIJj61dQgK6j4AR4J7x5Z2Yf1c” |
| `geometry.location.lat` | 37.4224764 |
| `geometry.location.lng` | -122.0842499 |

If you’re working in JavaScript/Node.js, here’s a quick snippet using `fetch`:

```js
const fetch = require('node-fetch');

async function reverseGeocode(lat, lng) {
  const apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY';
  const url = `https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=${lat},${lng}&key=${apiKey}`;

  const res = await fetch(url);
  const data = await res.json();

  if (data.status === 'OK') {
    console.log(data.results[0].formatted_address); // e.g., “1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA”
  } else {
    console.error('Error:', data.status);
  }
}

reverseGeocode(37.4224764, -122.0842499);
```

That’s all you need to confirm the coordinates and pull back the human‑readable address or other metadata.
